## ABSTRACT
Although antibodies remain a primary recognition element in all forms of biosensing, functional limitations arising from their size, stability, and structure have motivated the development and production of many different artificial scaffold proteins for biological recognition. However, implementing such artificial binders into functional high-performance biosensors remains a challenging task. Here, we present the design and application of Förster resonance energy transfer (FRET) nanoprobes comprised of small artificial proteins (αRep bidomains) labeled with a Tb complex (Tb) donor on the C-terminus and a semiconductor quantum dot (QD) acceptor on the N-terminus. Specific binding of one or two protein targets to the αReps induced a conformational change that could be detected by time-resolved Tb-to-QD FRET. These single-probe FRET-switches were used in a separation-free solution-phase assay to quantify different protein targets at subnanomolar concentrations and measure the conformational changes with subnanometer resolution. Probing ligand-receptor binding under physiological conditions at very low concentrations in solution is a special feature of FRET that can be efficiently combined with other structural characterization methods to develop, understand, and optimize artificial biosensors. Our results suggest that the αRep FRET nanoprobes have a strong potential for their application in advanced diagnostics and intracellular live cell imaging of ligand-receptor interactions.
## KEYWORDS
FRET, lanthanides, molecular diagnostics, molecular ruler, protein structure, binding assay
Antibodies are arguably the most applied biological recognition molecules and immunoassays have been developed for a multitude of biological targets with a large variety of technologies.\(^{1}\) Despite their broad application, antibodies possess several disadvantages, such as relatively large size, heterotetrameric structure, and disulfide bonds, which prevent efficient production as recombinant proteins in low cost prokaryotic expression systems. The architecture of immunoglobulin (Ig) domains found in antibodies results in high aggregation propensity, which considerably limits the design and production of engineered proteins using Ig domains as recognition units. Efforts to overcome the intrinsic limits of antibodies have led to the design of specific binding proteins based on alternative scaffolds.\(^{2-4}\) These artificial proteins are usually designed to be very robust, enabling construction and efficient production of a wide panel of fusion proteins. Among possible alternative scaffolds, proteins with repeated architectures, such as leucine rich repeats (LRRs), ankyrin (AR), tetratricopeptide (TPR), armadillo (ARM), and HEAT (Huntingtin, elongation factor 3 (EF3), protein phosphatase 2A (PP2A), and the yeast kinase TOR1) repeats, are particularly adapted to molecular recognition and artificial evolution.\(^{5,6}\) Because a single, versatile architecture can be evolved to bind any predefined protein target, these artificial binders are very beneficial for generic biosensor design.\(^{7,8}\)
Owing to its nanometric distance-dependence, Förster Resonance Energy Transfer (FRET) is a powerful technique to measure distances and conformational changes in biological complexes and to quantify biological interactions, e.g., in immunoassays or DNA-hybridization assays.\(^{9-19}\) Binding a target with at least two different recognition molecules (e.g., in a sandwich immunoassay) can significantly increase target specificity. In a FRET assay format, the binders must be conjugated with a FRET donor and acceptor and the FRET pair must be in close enough distance (after target binding) for efficient FRET. These exigent requirements can be met for well-established binders, such as antibodies or nucleic acids, but have limited FRET biosensors with artificial scaffold proteins to only a few examples.\(^{20-23}\) We have
recently developed a strategy to convert the molecular recognition event between αReps\(^{24}\) (a family of artificial proteins composed of HEAT-like repeats) and their targets into a measurable readout for FRET biosensing.\(^{25}\) A library of \(\sim1.7 \times 10^{9}\) variants is available and represents a source of potential binders for any protein target of interest.\(^{26-31}\) Each αRep has a specific binding surface made of the juxtaposition of hypervariable residues, which can be used to tightly and specifically bind to predefined targets in a precise orientation. αRep's six repeats (two terminal capping motifs, and four internal variable motifs) can form a "closed" structure similar to a closed bivalve shell when connected together.\(^{25}\) Importantly, this closed form does not prevent target binding and we previously observed that the bivalve can open upon ligand binding, with each of the two αRep domains binding its specific target. Hexahistidine (His\(_6\)) and cysteine (Cys) tags on the N- and C-termini of the bidomain sequence allowed for conjugating a dye FRET-pair on the bidomain αRep and to monitor target binding. However, the low affinity of nitrilotriacetic acid (NTA)-functionalized dyes to the His\(_6\) tag\(^{32,33}\) required micromolar concentrations of bidomain αRep and dyes,\(^{25}\) which is several orders of magnitude too large for most biosensing applications. While it should be possible to reengineer the αRep bidomain toward orthogonal coupling of a dye FRET pair on the N- and C-termini, respectively, it appears much more beneficial to use the His\(_6\) tag for both isolation of the expressed protein and efficient self-assembly to the surface of ZnS-shelled semiconductor quantum dots (QDs) with nanomolar affinity.\(^{34,35}\) Such an approach could become applicable to ratiometric protein sensing via perturbation of QD-dye FRET.\(^{36}\)
Here, we combine efficient His\(_6\)-QD self-assembly with the outstanding performance of terbium-to-QD FRET for ultrasensitive quantification of different biological targets\(^{37-43}\) and long distance conformational analysis.\(^{44}\) Despite the relatively large size of QDs and proteins, we demonstrate that Tb-to-QD FRET can accomplish conformational analysis at subnanometer resolution beyond DNA and peptides. By conjugating a Tb donor to a single Cys on the C-terminus and a QD acceptor to a single His\(_6\)-
tag on the N-terminus of the αRep bidomain, we were able to measure the opening of the bivalve protein upon binding of one or two targets in solution, under physiological conditions, at low nanomolar concentrations of the αReps, and subnanomolar concentrations of QDs. In addition to this demonstration of a QD-based FRET molecular ruler for protein conformation analysis, we could use the single QDαRep-Tb conjugate to develop simple homogeneous (separation-free) binding assays for two different protein targets. The assays provided subnanomolar limits of detection (LODs) in PBS buffer and low nanomolar LODs in serum-containing samples. Moreover, we could follow the sequential binding of the two different protein targets by a single bivalve αRep protein.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Principle of Tb-to-QD-FRET biosensor. We recently described two artificial bidomain proteins, constructed by connecting two αRep proteins named A3 or one A3 and another αRep named bGFPD with a flexible peptide. These construct yielded the homo-bidomain "A3_A3" and the hetero-bidomain "A3_bGFPD".<sup>25</sup> Both bidomain αRep constructs were expressed as single (monomer) proteins and contained a His6-tag on the N-terminus and a Cys on the C-terminus. The A3 domain specifically binds the target protein bA3-2 (another αRep that was selected by phage display against A3) whereas the bGFPD domain binds the fluorescent proteins eGFP (enhanced green fluorescent protein) or BFP (blue fluorescent protein). We previously showed that each domain can bind its targets (A3_A3 with two bA3-2 and A3_bGFPD with one bA3-2 and one eGFP or BFP) with high affinity (\(K_{\mathrm{D}} \sim 50~\mathrm{nM}\)).<sup>25</sup> Without targets, both bidomains remain in a "closed" conformation with both domains facing each other. Upon target binding, the bidomains switch to an "open" state, whose conformation depends on the number of bound ligands (one or two). This behaviour (closed/open state) is generic for all αReps displaying the same number of repeats and can therefore be adapted to other ligands than the two prototypical target proteins
used in our present study. Both bidomain proteins were conjugated with a Lumi4-Tb complex FRET donor (Tb)\(^{45}\) and a DHLA-PEG-OMe coated \(625\;\mathrm{nm}\) emitting QD FRET acceptor (QD).\(^{46-50}\) Tb was specifically grafted (coupling efficiency of \(\sim90\;\%\)) through a maleimide thiol coupling on the single Cys located at the C-terminus of each bidomain protein. The Tb-conjugated bidomain was then self-assembled to the Zn-rich QD surface via its N-terminal \(\mathrm{His}_{6}\) (Supporting Figure S1).\(^{51}\) While the extended form of DHLA-PEG-OMe displays a length of \(\sim6.8\;\mathrm{nm}\), it adapts an energy minimized conformation of \(\sim3.7\;\mathrm{nm}\) on the QD surface.\(^{52}\) Additionally, DHLA-PEG-OMe does not block the QD and there is still sufficient space for \(\mathrm{His}_{6}\)-tagged proteins to access the ZnS surface of the QD as long as the protein is not too large and the \(\mathrm{His}_{6}\) is extended out of the protein.\(^{53}\) Our αRep bidomains are relatively small and were expressed with an N-terminal \(\mathrm{His}_{6}\) that is extended on a nine-residues (TENLYFQGA) linker, which ensured efficient self-assembly of the Tb-conjugated bidomain to the QD. Self-assembly of engineered scaffold proteins to QD surfaces via \(\mathrm{His}_{8}\) or \(\mathrm{His}_{6}\) tags and the use of these QD-protein conjugates in FRET bioassays has been previously demonstrated.\(^{20,23}\) A maximum of 10 to 15 proteins per QD was obtained, which is in agreement with previous findings of attaching 12 maltose binding proteins (a protein with similar molecular weight of 44kDa) per QD.\(^{54}\) To avoid steric hindrance on the QD surface and allow efficient dynamic conformation change upon ligand binding, we limited the protein/QD ratio to 3 for the ligand binding assays. Photophysical properties, FRET parameters, and bioconjugation details are presented in the Materials and Methods section.
Figure 1 shows the principle of measuring ligand concentrations and bidomain conformations by changes in FRET between the Tb donor (on the C-terminus of the αRep bidomains) and the QD acceptor (on the N-terminus of the αRep bidomains) as the bidomain protein changes its structure upon a binding event. In total, we investigated four different configurations, namely A3_A3 bidomain with bA3-2 ligand (Figure 1A), A3_bGFPD bidomain with bA3-2 ligand (Figure 1B), A3_bGFPD bidomain with BFP
ligand (Figure 1C), and A3_bGFPD bidomain with both bA3-2 and BFP ligands (Figure 1D). For better illustration, schematics of the closed (without targets) and open (with targets) conformations are shown in Figures 1E and F. The amount of target determines the amount of opened bidomain αReps on the QD surface and thus, the fractions of closed, single-target-opened, and dual-target-opened conformations. The FRET efficiency quantifies the Tb-QD distance (FRET efficiency is inversely proportional to the Tb-QD distance to the power of six) or bidomain conformation, whereas the FRET intensity quantifies the concentration of each conformation (ratio of QD and Tb PL intensity is proportional to the number of FRET pairs) and thus the concentration of targets. Despite the relatively large QD radius of circa \(4.6\;\mathrm{nm}\) and the size of each αRep domain (13.7 kDa) of circa \(5.5\;\mathrm{nm} \times 2.5\;\mathrm{nm}\) (when assimilated as a cylinder – cf. pdb structure 6HWP), the exceptionally large Förster distance (Tb-to-QD-center distance for which FRET is \(50\;\%\) efficient) of \(R_{0} = 10\;\mathrm{nm}\) (vide supra) provides this lanthanide-QD FRET system with the possibility of quantifying the opening and closing of the bidomain proteins with subnanometer resolution.
Time-resolved and time-gated FRET analysis. In order to analyze both ligand concentrations and αRep bidomain conformations in solution, in small volumes (150 \(\upmu\)L), and at low concentrations (few nM), we relied on time-resolved fluorescence plate readers. Upon illumination of the samples with a pulsed (20 Hz) nitrogen laser (337.1 nm), both the Tb donor and the QD acceptor were efficiently excited. Owing to the very large difference in excited-state lifetimes (milliseconds for Tb and nanoseconds for QD), close to 100\,\% of the Tb donors remained in their excited states after the QDs had decayed back to their ground states. Thus, after a short time delay of a few \(\upmu\)s, Tb-QD became a very efficient FRET pair
and the FRET-quenched PL decay time of the Tb donor became the same as the PL decay time of the QD acceptor \(\left(\tau_{\mathrm{DA}}=\tau_{\mathrm{AD}}\right)\).<sup>43</sup> Both Tb (monitored at \(494 \pm 12\ \mathrm{nm}\)) and QD (monitored at \(639 \pm 10\ \mathrm{nm}\)) PL decays and time-gated intensities (in a time-window ranging from 0.05 to 0.95 ms after the excitation pulse) were acquired. An example of the measured Tb and QD PL decay curves of Tb-conjugated A3_A3 (without ligand) in the presence of QDs at six different concentrations is shown in Figures 2A and B.
We first investigated all systems (both A3_A3 and A3_bGFPD with and without target ligands) at constant bidomain concentrations (4 nM) but different QD concentrations (between \(0.03\ \mathrm{nM}\) and 1.33 nM). Analyzing the \(\alpha\)Rep bidomains with and without bound ligands at many different QD concentrations was performed due to two important reasons. First, it allowed us to evaluate Tb-QD distances and the corresponding protein conformations at various protein/QD ratios, thus providing a large data set for combined decay-time and FRET analysis. Second, we could determine the best protein/QD conjugation ratios for single-bidomain ligand binding assays. Linearly increasing FRET-ratios (ratios of time-gated PL intensities of QD and Tb) with increasing QD concentrations (i.e., decreasing protein-to-QD ratios) demonstrated efficient self-assembly of the N-terminal \(\mathrm{His}_{6}\)-tag (independent of the \(\alpha\)Rep bidomain and the bound ligands) to the QDs (Figure 2C and D). These results showed that the QD-surface attachment and FRET between Tb and QD were functional even at relatively high protein densities. When the density decreased below 15 to 10 proteins per QD, FRET-ratios did not continue to significantly increase because additional QDs (in combination with the constant Tb concentration) did not result in additional FRET pairs (i.e., "\(x\) Tb on 1 QD" and "\(x\) QDs with 1 Tb each" both represent \(x\) Tb-to-QD FRET pairs because one QD can be FRET-sensitized by several Tb sequentially). Although the QD-surface assembly of bidomain-ligand complexes (assembly to the QD after ligand binding) was not problematic for high protein densities, ligand binding by the \(\alpha\)Rep bidomains on the QD surface (assembly to the QD before
## Single-bidomain ligand binding assays. For protein quantification via a single Tb-bidomain-QD
sensor (instead of two different receptors as usually applied for sandwich immunoassays), we prepared samples containing \(1.33\;\mathsf{n M}\) of FRET-probe ( \(1.33\;\mathsf{n M}\) QD conjugated to \(4\;\mathsf{n M}\) of αRep-bidomain-Tb) in \(150~\upmu\mathrm{L}\). Assay calibration curves (FRET-ratio as a function of target concentration) were recorded for
target concentrations between \(\sim100\;\mathrm{pM}\) and \(\sim300\;\mathrm{nM}\). All assay calibration curves (Figure 3A) showed the expected behavior of decreasing FRET-ratios with increasing target concentrations, caused by the opening of the bidomains and concomitantly reduced FRET (longer Tb-to-QD distance for opened bidomains). In other words, the more target ligands were present in the samples, the higher was the fraction of opened (low-FRET) FRET probes. All three biosensors (A3_A3 against bA3-2, Figure 3C; A3_bGFPD against bA3-2, Figure 3D; A3_bGFPD against BFP, Figure 3E) showed a steep decrease of the FRET-ratio for ligand concentrations below or in the order of the bidomain concentration of \(4\;\mathrm{nM}\) because of efficient bidomain opening via ligand-binding. At higher concentrations, the αRep bidomains became saturated and the FRET-ratio decreased less steeply. Full binding saturation was reached at ligand concentrations of approximately 30 to \(40\;\mathrm{nM}\). All three assays were performed in PBS buffer containing \(0.1\;\%\) BSA (Figure 3A and Supporting Figure S2) with LODs of \(300\pm20\;\mathrm{pM}\) \((4.2{\pm}0.3\;\mathrm{ng\;mL^{-1}})\) and \(500\pm30\;\mathrm{pM}\) \((6.8\pm0.4\;\mathrm{ng\;mL^{-1}})\) for bA3-2 with A3_A3 and A3_bGFPD, respectively, and \(740\pm40\;\mathrm{pM}\) \((23\pm2\;\mathrm{ng\;mL^{-1}})\) for BFP with A3_bGFPD, which demonstrated the excellent diagnostic performance of these QD-based artificial-protein biosensors. To go a step further toward a real clinical sample, we challenged the bA3-2 assays with samples containing \(15\;\%\) fetal bovine serum. For both FRET-probes, the calibration curves showed a similar behavior as for samples in PBS (Supporting Figure S3) and LODs of \(1.9\pm0.2\;\mathrm{nM}\) \((26\pm3\;\mathrm{ng\;mL^{-1}})\) and \(7.4\pm0.8\;\mathrm{nM}\) \((104\pm11\;\mathrm{ng\;mL^{-1}})\) bA3-2 for A3_A3 and A3_bGFPD, respectively. Taking into account that the aim of our study was not assay optimization but a proof-of-concept for using an artificial nanobiosensor composed of single bidomains attached to a QD, this assay performance is rather impressive and suggest that these artificial proteins are as specific as the best conventional antibodies and are not displaced by very high concentrations of non-relevant serum proteins. Improvements of the assay should be feasible by increasing the ligand-binding induced distance variation within the bidomains and, specifically, by decreasing the FRET efficiency of the open form. As
demonstrated in the conformational study below, the minimal FRET efficiency observed for the ligand-bound conformation was still relatively high (49\%). Possible improvements could be expected by adjusting the amino acid composition of the linker to extend the ligand-bound form. It was indeed shown that a linker rich in prolines tends to adopt more extended and stiffer conformations.\(^{55}\) However, the linker needs to retain at least some flexibility to still allow the closed conformation of the bidomains. Such considerations and relations of assay performance and protein conformation show the importance of developing biosensors that can be used as concentration and distance probes within the same assay.
In addition to a simplified assay format (separation-free in-solution assay with only one binding protein), another interesting property of the αRep bidomains is their affinity for two targets (each domain binds one target), which can even be different, as in the case of A3_bGFPD. To evaluate if A3_bGFPD is capable of detecting bA3-2 and BFP sequentially, we prepared assays in which we first added bA3-2 and then BFP and vice versa. While the sequential assay could be performed in either way, the influence of BFP addition was significantly larger, no matter if BFP was added first or second (Figure 3B). These sensor responses followed very well the single-target assays (Figure 3A), for which a significantly stronger signal change was found for BFP (\(\sim\)60\% decrease) compared to bA3-2 (\(\sim\)20\% decrease). This difference in FRET response may also indicate that larger BFP resulted in a significantly wider opening of the αRep bidomain compared to bA3-2, which was further investigated by the conformational FRET study (vide infra). Although simultaneous quantification of both targets will not be straightforward with such a dual-specific single-protein FRET-probe, the dual-step opening of the bidomain is a special property that may lead to the design of special sensing concepts, in particular for kinetic measurements, in which a single probe for investigating sequential ligand-receptor interactions may be an interesting tool.
Conformational analysis of ligand binding. To obtain a better insight into the open (with ligand) and closed (without ligand) conformations of the αRep bidomains, we analyzed the PL decays of the different systems with the aim to quantify the Tb-QD distances by FRET (see Materials and Methods part for detailed analytical procedure) and relate these distances to the structures of the functional αRep bidomains on the QDs. Although both the FRET quenching of the Tb donor (Figure 2A and Supporting Figures S4 to S9 left) and FRET sensitization of the QD acceptor (Figure 2B and Supporting Figures S4 to S9 right) could be clearly observed, the extremely high brightness of the QDs combined with excitation at \(337~\mathrm{nm}\) (extinction coefficient of \(\sim1\!\times\!10^{7}\,\mathrm{M^{-1}cm^{-1}}\)) resulted in significant background PL (Supporting Figure S10) even in the ms time range (mainly caused by saturation of the detection system). On the other hand, the Tb PL was significantly FRET-quenched without QD background PL and we therefore used only the Tb PL decays for distance analysis. In order to work on a sufficiently large data
set for statistically reliable results, we used 13 different samples with QD concentrations ranging from 0.03 to \(1.33\ \mathrm{nM}\) for FRET measurements. For all samples, the concentration of bidomain-Tb was kept constant at \(4\ \mathrm{nM}\). Ligand-containing samples included ligands at 250-fold excess \((1\ \upmu\mathrm{M})\) to guarantee that all αReps bind one ligand (open bidomain conformation). While all QD concentrations resulted in significant FRET quenching (Figure 2A), the three lowest concentrations had nominal protein/QD ratios higher than 30 and the overall FRET quenching was not sufficient to accomplish reliable fit results (cf. fit results in Supporting Figures S4 to S9). Thus, we selected the 10 highest concentrations (protein/QD ratios between 3 and 15) for FRET analysis. The average distances from the 10 samples of each configuration (Table 1) showed a clear opening (distance increase) of the αRep-bidomains upon ligand binding. The FRET results were then used for modeling the system (Figure 4) by taking into account structural results of the bound and unbound αRep bidomains obtained by combined size exclusion chromatography and multi-angle light scattering (SEC-MALS) and small angle X-ray scattering (SAXS) from a previous study.25
The obtained distances were well suited to explain the orientation of the αRep bidomains on the QD surface and their conformation during open and closed states. In the unbound state (closed), the Tb-
QD distance is very similar for both A3_A3 (3.3±0.4 nm) and A3_bGFPD (3.7±0.4 nm), which is reasonable because for both bidomains, the A3 αRep was attached (via His6) to the QD surface, the linker is the same for both bidomains, and bGFPD has a very similar size compared to A3. The distances also indicate that the bidomains are closed and oriented tangentially on the QD surface (cf. Figure 4A and B left). The distances of the bound states (open) reveal rather interesting information concerning the bidomain conformations, which could become very useful for possible optimization of the FRET probes. The A3_A3 bidomain opens by 1.8±0.2 nm compared to the closed state. This opening corresponds to the binding of two bA3-2 ligands (one by each A3) and to slightly more than twice the distance of only one bound bA3-2 (~0.8±0.1 nm), as measured for the interaction of A3_bGFPD with bA3-2. Thus, it can be assumed that the opening of A3_A3 by two bA3-2 is symmetric (two very similar opening steps), which could be expected due to the identical A3 αReps and bA3-2 ligands, and that the binding of one bA3-2 is slightly tighter than binding two bA3-2. For A3_bGFPD, the FRET data showed that the opening via sequential binding of bA3-2 and BFP was not symmetric at all (Table 1 and Figure 4B). Binding of bA3-2 resulted in an opening of 0.8±0.1 nm, whereas binding of BFP opened the bidomain by 1.8±0.2 nm. Interestingly, when both bA3-2 and BFP were bound, the opening distance was also 1.8±0.2 nm. These conformational changes confirmed the binding assays (vide supra), for which much stronger quenching of the FRET signal was observed upon BFP binding (Figure 3). Significant FRET quenching upon bA3-2 binding could only be observed when bA3-2 was bound first (red data points in Figure 3B), whereas FRET quenching was very weak when bA3-2 was bound second and BFP was bound first (blue data points in Figure 3B). Therefore, we interpret the unchanged distance of single BFP or combined BFP-bA3-2 ligand binding in A3-bGFPD as unsymmetrical opening, i.e., the two αReps rather rotate around the connecting ligand without changing the distance between C- and N-terminus of the bidomain when both BFP and bA3-2 are bound. Because BFP is significantly larger than bA3-2 (30.7 kDa vs. 13.7 kDa), its
binding may cause the major contribution to the conformational change, against which the addition of bA3-2 can be neglected. In this context, it would also be interesting to investigate the inverse attachment of QD (to the C-terminus) and Tb (to the N-terminus) and the replacement of the QD by a smaller acceptor (e.g., a fluorescent dye) to verify that the dynamic binding is not altered by the QD nanoparticle. However, such configurations would have required the design and selection of additional bidomain proteins and additional conjugation procedures and FRET experiments, which were out of the scope of the current study but will certainly be beneficial for optimizing these FRET probes in the future.
To further validate the conformational information determined via FRET (Figure 4), we calculated the hydrodynamic radii \((R_{\mathrm{h}})\) of both bidomains in the absence and in the presence of ligands. Using the distance restraints provided by the FRET measurements, the known structures of each αRep bidomain and their relative positions (with and without ligand) could be adjusted in a molecular model (cf. Materials and Methods for details). These modeled orientations could then be used to calculate hydrodynamic radii of the bidomains and compare them with results from SEC-MALS analysis (Table 1). For all structure models, the calculated \(R_{\mathrm{h}}\) were in very good agreement with the experimental data obtained by SEC-MALS.
A final important point derived from the FRET distance study concerned the extent of bidomain opening. Taking into account that each αRep domain is approximately \(5.5\;\mathrm{nm}\) in length, a much longer Tb-QD distance could have been expected, with a maximum extent of \(\sim10\;\mathrm{nm}\) in the case of fixed and radial orientation. When considering the flexibility of the αRep-attachment to the QD (His6 tag), the flexibility of both protein domains in themselves, and the flexibility of the connecting linker, it becomes clear that an actual measurement of distances is very important and superior to simple assumptions. Given the flexibility of the bidomains in the presence of their ligands, determination of conformational data by classical approaches of structural analysis would be very difficult to impossible. In contrast, despite the
relatively large size of QDs (compared to most biomolecules), Tb-to-QD FRET can provide nanometer (opening of \(1.8 \pm 0.2\) nm for "bA3-2/A3_A3/bA3-2", "A3_bGFPD/BFP", and "bA3-2/A3_bGFPD/BFP") to subnanometer (opening of \(0.8 \pm 0.1\) nm for "bA3-2/A3_bGFPD") spatial resolution and it can do so on a dynamic biomolecular system at low nanomolar concentrations in solution.
FRET cannot answer all conformational questions of these bidomain systems, such as "Why is the distance increased by only a few nanometers?", "Which part(s) of the protein bidomain structure introduce(s) the majority of flexibility?", or "What is the exact reason for a constant distance for BFP and BFP-bA3-2 binding in A3_bGFPD?". Also, other structural methods, such as electron microscopy or SAXS, may provide significantly better spatial resolution. However, FRET is the only tool that can measure distances at low concentrations and physiological conditions that can go as far as being exactly the same conditions as the actual application environment of the biosensor. The use of the Tb-to-QD FRET system provides other benefits to the system, such as simple and specific bioconjugation via His\(_6\) and Cys tags and high sensitivity (low concentrations).
## CONCLUSIONS
Although antibodies continue to be the most applied biorecognition molecules for molecular diagnostics, their physical disadvantages, including size, stability, structure, and production, have resulted in strong research efforts toward the development of alternative artificial binders. Small scaffold proteins are particularly interesting because they are very robust and can be efficiently produced from a wide panel of fusion proteins. αReps, scaffold proteins based on HEAT-like repeated motifs, provide a specific binding surface made of the juxtaposition of hypervariable residues and their large library of \(\sim1.7 \times 10^{9}\) variants is a rich source of potential binders for many protein targets. However, the advantages of αReps for molecular diagnostics have remained largely unexploited. In this study, we have combined the outstanding properties of Tb-to-QD FRET, as primarily related to simple and sensitive binding assays and subnanometer-resolution distance determination, with the special ligand-binding capabilities of small homo- and hetero-bidomain αReps. Simple and specific conjugation of Tb FRET donors and QD FRET acceptors via single Cys and His\(_6\) tags to the C and N termini of the same bidomain αReps resulted in
biomolecular FRET probes that could efficiently sense conformational changes in the bidomain. Specific binding of one or two ligands to the αReps resulted in the opening of the closed bidomains, which induced a significant reduction of the Tb-to-QD FRET signal. Using this single FRET-probe detection principle, we designed and applied separation-free binding assays for two different model protein targets (the αRep bA3-2 and the fluorescent protein BFP). High diagnostic performance was demonstrated by quantifying low nanomolar target concentrations in \(150~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) samples on the clinically approved fluorescence plate reader system KRYPTOR with LODs down to \(0.3\;\mathrm{nM}\) (4.2 ng/mL bA3-2) in PBS buffer and \(1.9\;\mathrm{nM}\) (26 ng/mL bA3-2) in serum-containing samples. Interestingly, the A3_bGFPD hetero-bidomain could bind both different targets, which could be quantified in an assay with sequential addition of both targets, no matter which of the targets was added first. As expected, the larger target (BFP) induced a stronger FRET signal change than the smaller target (bA3-2), most probably due to a wider opening of the heterobidomain by BFP. Time-resolved PL investigation of the same assays (same samples) allowed us to quantify Tb-to-QD distances inside the bidomain proteins and thus to structurally analyze the closed and open bidomain conformations and compare the results to the protein structures previously elucidated by SEC-MALS and SAXS. The FRET distances revealed that the A3_A3 homo-bidomain could bind two bA3-2 ligands, which resulted in an opening of the bidomain and an increase of the Tb-to-QD-surface distance by \(\sim1.8\;\mathrm{nm}\). The distance increase of \(\sim0.8\;\mathrm{nm}\) found for the same FRET-pair within the A3_bGFPD hetero-bidomain when bound to bA3-2, showed that the opening of A3_A3 by two bA3-2 consists of two approximately symmetric steps, as expected due to the same two A3-bA3-2 αRep-ligand pairs. The structural FRET analysis also confirmed the significantly larger opening of A3_bGFPD by BFP. In fact, BFP alone resulted in a distance increase of \(\sim1.8\;\mathrm{nm}\) and this distance remained the same if both BFP and bA3-2 were bound, which indicated that the binding of bA3-2 alone resulted in only a very
small distance increase (most probably a rotation in reference to the QD surface rather than an opening), as already found for the much lower signal change in the FRET binding assays.
The simple analysis of conformational changes by FRET under direct binding assay conditions presents a powerful tool for designing and optimizing assays because it gives direct access to the distances in the closed and open forms. Knowing the protein structures (from other analytical methods), these distances can be used to help with probe optimization, e.g., increasing or decreasing the length of the bidomain linkers or expressing the His\(_6\) and Cys tags at different positions within the αRep bidomain. Our Tb-to-QD FRET αRep ligand binding probe for simultaneous high sensitivity sensing and high spatial-resolution conformational analysis may also become extremely useful for intracellular ligand binding studies, a challenge that cannot be easily met by the disulfide-containing IgG antibodies. We therefore believe that these αRep nano-FRET probes will provide important advances to research and applications for both in-vitro liquid-phase sensing and in-situ live cell imaging.
## MATERIALS AND METHODS
Protein expression and purification. Expression and purification of mono His-tagged αRep bidomains and bA3-2 and BFP target proteins were performed as described elsewhere.\(^{26,31}\) For both bidomain proteins the encoding sequences were cloned into an expression pQE plasmid with the N-terminal sequence "MRGSHHHHHHTENLYFQGA". The E. coli Gold strain was transformed with the plasmid encoding for the different proteins. Cells were grown at \(37^{\circ}\,\mathrm{C}\) in 2YT medium containing \(200~\upmu\mathrm{g.L^{-1}}\) ampicillin to an absorbance of 0.6 at \(600\;\mathrm{nm}\). Protein expression was induced by addition of \(1~\mathrm{mM}\) IPTG and the cells were further incubated for \(5\;\mathrm{h}\) at \(37^{\circ}\,\mathrm{C}\). Then cells were harvested, suspended in PBS (50 mM sodium phosphate, 150 mM NaCl, pH7.4), submitted to three freezing/thawing cycles, treated \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) with DNase I and sonicated. His-tagged αRep bidomain proteins were purified from crude supernatant
using nickel-affinity chromatography (Histrap™ FF crude 5mL, GE Healthcare) followed by size-exclusion chromatography (Hiload 16/600 Superdex™ 75) in PBS. Twin-Strep-tagged target proteins were purified from crude supernatant using a StrepTactin Sepharose affinity chromatography (StrepTrap™ HP 5mL, GE Healthcare) followed by size exclusion chromatography under the same conditions as mentioned above. For each protein, the purity of the final sample was checked by SDS–PAGE with an overloaded gel showing one well-resolved band with no visible contamination. For all the following experiments the proteins were quantified by UV spectrophotometry and expressed in monomer concentration.
QD-protein conjugation. Tb-conjugated proteins were self-assembled to QDs (625 nm emitting proprietary QDs, Invitrogen by Life Technologies, coated with DHLA-PEG-OMe (methoxy-terminated
dihydrolipoic acid-appended poly(ethylene glycol)\(^{33,53,56}\) via a single N-terminal His\(_6\) on the bidomain αReps by mixing QD and αRep at the desired stoichiometric ratios in PBS for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) at room temperature. The His\(_6\) tag followed by the nine-residues linker (TENLYFQGA), is sufficiently extended to self-assemble the protein to the ZnS QD surface across the DHLA-PEG-OMe ligand. The well characterized, high-affinity binding resulted in approximately quantitative QD-protein assembly and thus, no purification was necessary. Protein per QD ratios and donor/acceptor ratios were quantified by Tb, protein, and QD absorption (see Figure 5 for QD extinction coefficients).
Photophysical properties and FRET parameters of Tb and QD. The photoluminescence (PL) decay of Tb conjugated to the αRep was biexponential with an average lifetime (or donor lifetime) of \(\tau_{\mathrm{D}}=2.52 \pm 0.05\;\mathrm{ms}\) (one major component of \(2.7\;\mathrm{ms}\) and one minor component in the \(0.4\) to \(0.8\;\mathrm{ms}\) range). The QD has an excited state lifetime in the \(50\;\mathrm{ns}\) range.\(^{57,58}\) The PL spectra of Tb and QD, the absorption spectrum of QD, and the detection channel (Tb and QD) filter transmission spectra are shown in Figure 5. These transmission filters (\(494 \pm 12\;\mathrm{nm}\) for Tb and \(640 \pm 10\;\mathrm{nm}\) for QD, both from Semrock) were also used on an Edinburgh Instruments fluorescence lifetime plate reader to record PL decay curves of Tb donors and QD acceptors using 4000 detection bins of \(2\;\upmu\mathrm{s}\) integration time and nitrogen laser (MNL 100, LTB Lasertechnik Berlin) excitation (\(337.1\;\mathrm{nm}, 20\;\mathrm{Hz}\)). Decays were fit with fluorescence lifetime analysis software "fast" from Edinburgh Instruments.
The Förster distance \( R_{0} \) corresponds to the donor-acceptor distance for which FRET efficiency is \( 50\% \). For the Tb–QD FRET pair (inside the αRep bidomain) \( R_{0} = 10 \pm 1 \, \mathrm{nm} \), which was calculated using Equation 1:
\[
R_{0}=0.021(\kappa^{2}\Phi_{D}n^{-4}J)^{1/6}\qquad(\mathrm{in~nm})
\]
with the orientation factor \( \kappa^{2} = 2/3 \) (dynamic averaging due to random orientation of the donor and acceptor during FRET), the Tb donor quantum yield \( \Phi_{\mathrm{D}} = 0.81 \pm 0.05 \), the refractive index \( n = 1.35 \) (aqueous solution) and the overlap integral \( J = 8.03 \times 10^{16} \, \mathrm{M}^{-1} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \, \mathrm{nm}^{4} \) as calculated by Equation 2:
\[
\begin{array}{r}J=\int_{450}^{650}I_D(\lambda)\varepsilon_A(\lambda)\lambda^4d\lambda\end{array}
\]
where \( I_{\mathrm{D}}(\lambda) \) is the emission intensity from the area-normalized (to unity) emission spectrum of the Tb donor and \( \varepsilon_{\mathrm{A}}(\lambda) \) is the molar absorptivity from the absorption spectrum of the QD acceptor.
Notably, the fluorescent proteins eGFP and BFP, which were target ligands in our study, are also fluorophores and may therefore also engage in FRET. Owing to the spectral overlap of Tb emission and eGFP absorption but the lack of overlap between Tb emission and BFP absorption (Supporting Figure S11), we selected BFP instead of eGFP as a model target for the A3_bGFPD bidomain.
\[
\begin{array}{r}I=\sum_{i}A_{i-fit}*\exp\left(-\frac{t-t_{0}}{\tau_{i}}\right)=\sum_{i}A_{i}*\exp\left(-\frac{t}{\tau_{i}}\right)\end{array}
\]
Because the actual decay starts before \(t_{0}\), the amplitudes \((\mathbf{A}_{i-f i t})\), which are used to calculate average PL lifetimes, need to be corrected using Equation 4:
\[
\begin{array}{r}I=A_{i-fit}*\exp\left(-\frac{t-t_0}{\tau}\right)=A_i*\exp\left(-\frac{t}{\tau}\right)\Rightarrow A_i=A_{i-fit}*\exp\left(\frac{t_0}{\tau}\right)\end{array}
\]
Data corresponding to Tb donor alone (in the absence of QD acceptor) was fit using a double-exponential decay with a freely fit short decay time \(\tau_{\mathrm{x}}\) (amplitude \(\alpha_{\mathrm{x}}\)) and a fixed long decay time \(\tau_{0}=2.7\,\mathrm{ms}\) (freely fit amplitude \(\alpha_{0}\)), which led to an amplitude averaged donor decay time \((\tau_{\mathrm{D}})\). The Tb donor PL decay
\[
\begin{array}{r}\alpha_{DA1}=\frac{\alpha_{DA*1}}{\alpha_{DA*1}+\alpha_{DA*2}}\quad\mathrm{and}\quad\alpha_{DA2}=\frac{\alpha_{DA*2}}{\alpha_{DA*1}+\alpha_{DA*2}}\end{array}
\]
Since the unquenched donor also possessed a weak but significant short decay time \(\left(\tau_{\mathrm{x}}\right)\) that falls into the same time range as the two FRET decay times, another correction, which corresponds to the fraction of unquenched donor in the short time component (Equation 6), is required.
\[
\begin{array}{r}z_{D}=\alpha_{x}\left(\frac{\alpha_{0^{*}}}{\alpha_{0}}\right)\end{array}
\]
The average FRET decay time is then:
\[
\begin{array}{r}\tau_{DA}=\frac{\alpha_{DA1}\tau_{DA1}+\alpha_{DA2}\tau_{DA2}-z_D(\alpha_{DA1}+\alpha_{DA2})\tau_x}{\alpha_{DA1}+\alpha_{DA2}-z_D(\alpha_{DA1}+\alpha_{DA2})}=\frac{\alpha_{DA1}\tau_{DA1}+\alpha_{DA2}\tau_{DA2}-z_D\tau_x}{1-z_D}\end{array}
\]
The average FRET efficiency \(\left(E_{\mathrm{FRET}}\right)\) and average donor-acceptor distance \((R)\) can then be calculated using Equations 8 and 9:
\[
\begin{array}{r}E_{FRET}=\frac{1}{1+\left(\frac{R}{R_0}\right)^6}=1-\frac{\tau_{DA}}{\tau_D}\end{array}
\]
\[
\begin{array}{r}R=R_0*\sqrt{\frac{1-\tau_{FRET}}{E_{FRET}}}=R_0*\sqrt{\frac{\tau_{DA}}{\tau_D-\tau_{DA}}}\end{array}
\]
Homogeneous FRET binding assays and LOD determination. The Tb-protein conjugates and QDs were each dissolved in \(50~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of PBS with final concentrations of \(12\;\mathrm{nM}\) and \(4\;\mathrm{nM}\), respectively, and incubated during \(4\,\mathrm{h}\). \(50~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of ligand with varying concentrations were added to the \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) solutions
containing the Tb-αRep-QD conjugates. Ligands were either in PBS or in PBS containing \(40\,\%\) serum (resulting in \(15\,\%\) serum in the final sample). The final concentrations of labelled protein and QD were \(4\,\mathrm{nM}\) and \(1.33\,\mathrm{nM}\), respectively. After sample preparation, the microplates were incubated for 15 minutes at room temperature before measurements. Time-gated \((0.05-0.95\,\mathrm{ms})\) PL intensity measurements were acquired on a KRYPTOR compact plus fluorescence plate reader (Cezanne/Thermo Fisher Scientific) with nitrogen laser excitation \((337.1\,\mathrm{nm},\,20\,\mathrm{Hz},\,100\,\mathrm{pulses})\). Optical transmission filter bandpass wavelengths were \(494{\pm}12\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Semrock) for the Tb detection channel and \(640{\pm}10\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Semrock) for the QD625 detection channel. All FRET assays were measured in black 96-wells microtiter plates with an optimal working volume of \(150\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\). Each sample containing different concentrations of ligand (bA3-2 or BFP) was prepared in triplicates and samples without ligands (zero controls) were prepared 10 times. All samples were measured in triplicate. Error bars that are not visible in the graphs are smaller than the data points. Limits of detection (LOD) were estimated using Equation 10:
\[
LOD=\left|\frac{3*\sigma_{zero}}{\alpha}\right|
\]
with the standard deviation of the ten zero control samples \(\left(\sigma_{\mathrm{zero}}\right)\) measured in triplicates \((24\leq n\leq30\), for some systems, one or two samples had to be excluded from the analysis due to pipetting errors, i.e., outliers) and the slope of the linear regression \((\alpha)\) performed on the first 10 concentrations of ligands \((0-1.67\,\mathrm{nM}\) in PBS and \(0-14\,\mathrm{nM}\) in serum) for each assay.
Structural analysis of QD- αRep conjugates. Schematic representations of the protein complexes were obtained by combining available structural data with the calculated distances obtained by FRET. The structural models were designed using previously determined structural data available from the RCSB Protein Data Bank: X-ray structures of A3_A3 (PDB ID: 6FSQ), A3/bA3-2 complex (PDB ID: 4JW2), and a GFP/αRep binder complex (PDB ID: 4XL5) and a model of A3_bGFPD issued from SAXS data analysis (PDB ID: 6HWP)). For A3_A3, the linker between the two domains was positioned in the same
way as found in the A3-bGFPD SAXS model using ModLoop.\(^{59}\) QD625 was designed in Pymol as a sphere with a diameter of \(9.2\;\mathrm{nm}\).\(^{49,50}\) A3_A3 was positioned on the QD surface with the N-terminal His\(_6\) assembled to the QD surface and the C-terminal L4-Tb ligand positioned at a distance of \(3.3\;\mathrm{nm}\) from the surface as determined by FRET. The A3_bGFPD model obtained by SAXS was positioned by superimposing its A3 domain to the N-terminal domain of A3_A3. Using this approach, the resulting distance between L4-Tb of A3_bGFPD and the QD surface was in accordance with the one determined by FRET \((3.7\;\mathrm{nm})\). After positioning the two bidomains, ligands were added using the known structures (PDB IDs 4JW2 and 4XL5). Only the C-terminal domains were moved using a flexible hinge. Their positions were adjusted, avoiding steric hindrance and taking into account the distance determined by FRET. To quantitatively validate the FRET results, the hydrodynamic radii of the modelled molecules were calculated and compared with previously reported experimental SEC-MALS data. The N-terminal bidomain flexible peptides (His\(_6\) and linker) of each conformation were then built using ModLoop. The procedure was repeated five times, which resulted in five possible loop conformations. The hydrodynamic radii of each model in the absence and in the presence of the ligands were calculated using US-SOMO software\(^{60}\) and compared to those obtained by SEC-MALS.\(^{25}\) The hydrodynamic radius of A3_bGFPD/BFP was not modelled because it could not be determined with SEC-MALS (no comparative data available).
